exec(open('c1.py').read())
# ---------------- digestive
case('em-appendicitis','Abdominal pain that moves to the right lower side','digestive','digestive',
 'Explain why appendicitis pain starts around the navel and then moves to the right iliac fossa.',
 'A 17-year-old has had vague pain around the navel since last night. Now the pain is sharp in the right lower abdomen and he has a fever, no appetite and has vomited.',
 ['appendix','large-intestine','small-intestine'],
 'The appendix is a midgut structure, so early inflammation sends pain through visceral afferents that enter the spinal cord at T10, felt around the navel. When the inflamed appendix touches the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic nerve supply, the pain becomes sharp and localised over McBurney point (about one-third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the navel). The appendicular artery is an end artery, so blockage can lead to gangrene and perforation.',
 ['Seek urgent medical care. This is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise.','Do not give food or drink, in case surgery is needed. Small sips of water are acceptable if the wait is long.','Do not give laxatives or apply a hot-water bottle, and do not take strong painkillers on your own that could mask the signs. Use only what a doctor advises.','Note the time pain started, the temperature, vomiting and any other symptoms and tell the doctor.'],
 'Severe pain, fever, rigid abdomen, repeated vomiting, or sudden relief of pain followed by worsening (possible perforation) are danger signs.',
 [mc('At which spinal level is early appendix pain referred, felt around the navel?',['T6','T10','L1','S2'],1,'Midgut visceral afferents enter the cord at about T10, felt in the periumbilical region.','intermediate','Explain referred visceral pain'),
  mc('Which artery supplies the appendix?',['Appendicular artery (from the ileocolic artery)','Inferior mesenteric artery','Left colic artery','Right gastric artery'],0,'The appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery, is an end artery in the mesoappendix.','foundation','Name the arterial supply of the appendix')])
case('em-gi-bleed','Vomiting blood','digestive','digestive',
 'Relate the portal-systemic anastomosis at the lower oesophagus to bleeding oesophageal varices.',
 'A man with long-term liver disease vomits a large amount of bright red blood and becomes faint.',
 ['oesophagus','stomach','liver','duodenum'],
 'Veins of the lower oesophagus drain both into the left gastric vein (portal system) and into the azygos system (systemic). In portal hypertension, as in cirrhosis, blood is diverted through these veins, which swell into varices under a thin mucosa and can rupture. Bleeding from a peptic ulcer in the stomach or duodenum is the other common cause; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery.',
 ['Call the emergency number immediately.','Lay the person on their side (recovery position) so that blood does not enter the airway. If they are faint, lay them flat and raise their legs unless they are vomiting.','Give nothing by mouth. Keep them warm and still.','Keep a sample or note the amount of blood and any medicines taken (aspirin, anti-inflammatory drugs or blood thinners) for the doctors.','In hospital: fluid and blood replacement, medicines, and endoscopy to band the varices or treat the ulcer.'],
 'Vomiting blood, or black tarry stools with dizziness, is always an emergency.',
 [mc('Which vein of the lower oesophagus forms part of the portal-systemic anastomosis with the azygos system?',['Left gastric vein','Splenic vein','Inferior mesenteric vein','Hepatic vein'],0,'Oesophageal tributaries of the left gastric vein (portal) connect with the azygos veins (systemic).','intermediate','Name the portal-systemic anastomosis'),
  mc('How should a person who is vomiting blood be positioned?',['Flat on their back with the head tilted back','In the recovery position on their side','Standing','Sitting leaning back'],1,'The recovery position keeps the airway clear so that blood and vomit drain out.','foundation','Position a vomiting person')])
case('em-perforated-ulcer','Sudden severe upper abdominal pain','digestive','digestive',
 'Explain why a perforated duodenal ulcer causes generalised abdominal rigidity and shoulder-tip pain.',
 'A middle-aged man who takes anti-inflammatory tablets for arthritis has sudden, severe upper abdominal pain. His abdomen is rigid and he lies still, and the pain also seems to be in his right shoulder.',
 ['stomach','duodenum','liver','diaphragm'],
 'When a peptic ulcer perforates, stomach and duodenal contents spill into the peritoneal cavity. Irritation of the parietal peritoneum causes severe pain and reflex tightening of the abdominal wall muscles (a board-like abdomen). Gas and fluid under the diaphragm irritate the diaphragmatic peritoneum, which is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3-C5), so the pain is referred to the shoulder tip.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Treat as a surgical emergency.','Let the person lie in the position of greatest comfort, often with the knees drawn up.','Give nothing to eat or drink.','Do not give painkillers unless a clinician advises. Keep the person warm and watch for signs of shock (pale, sweaty, fast pulse).','In hospital: intravenous fluids and antibiotics, imaging, and surgery.'],
 'A rigid abdomen with severe pain is an emergency.',
 [mc('Which nerve explains shoulder-tip pain from irritation of the diaphragm?',['Vagus nerve','Phrenic nerve','Intercostal nerve','Ilioinguinal nerve'],1,'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) supplies the central diaphragm and its peritoneum; the pain is referred to the C3-C5 dermatomes at the shoulder tip.','intermediate','Explain referred pain'),
  tf('The parietal peritoneum is supplied by somatic nerves and gives well-localised pain.',True,'True. The parietal peritoneum has somatic innervation; the visceral peritoneum has autonomic afferents and gives poorly localised pain.','intermediate','Contrast parietal and visceral pain')])
# ---------------- urinary
case('em-renal-colic','Severe loin-to-groin pain','urinary','urinary',
 'Name the three sites where a kidney stone can lodge in the ureter and explain why the pain moves from the loin to the groin.',
 'A 35-year-old has sudden, severe, wave-like pain from the right loin to the groin. He cannot lie still and has vomited. He now has a fever.',
 ['kidney','ureter','renal-pelvis','urinary-bladder'],
 'Stones tend to lodge at the narrowest parts of the ureter: the pelviureteric junction, where the ureter crosses the pelvic brim over the common iliac vessels, and the vesicoureteric junction in the bladder wall. Stretching of the ureter sends pain through T10-L2, felt from the loin to the groin and the scrotum or labia. A blocked, infected kidney is an emergency because of the risk of sepsis.',
 ['Seek medical care the same day. Go to the emergency department if there is fever, shivering, vomiting, or only one kidney.','Drink water unless you are vomiting. Heat on the loin can help comfort.','Take simple pain relief only as directed by a doctor.','Strain the urine if advised so that a stone can be analysed.','In hospital: imaging (usually low-dose CT), pain relief, antibiotics when infected, and drainage by stent or nephrostomy if blocked and infected.'],
 'Fever or shivering with colic, a single kidney, or an inability to pass urine make this an emergency.',
 [mc('Which is a site of narrowing in the ureter?',['Where it crosses the pelvic brim over the iliac vessels','The renal papilla','The middle of the bladder','The membranous urethra'],0,'The three constrictions are the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim crossing and the vesicoureteric junction.','intermediate','Name the ureteric constrictions'),
  mc('Why is colic with fever treated as an emergency?',['The stone is moving faster','An infected, blocked kidney can cause sepsis','The pain is more severe with fever','The bladder is about to burst'],1,'Infected urine behind a blockage can quickly spread to the bloodstream.','intermediate','Recognise obstructed infected kidney')])
case('em-urinary-retention','Unable to pass urine','urinary','urinary',
 'Describe the parts of the male urethra and the sites where a catheter can meet resistance.',
 'A 70-year-old man has not passed urine for 12 hours. He has a painful, swollen lower abdomen and a constant urge to pass urine.',
 ['urinary-bladder','prostate','male-urethra','urethra'],
 'An enlarged prostate surrounds the prostatic urethra and can squeeze it shut. The bladder fills and stretches, and the pain is carried by pelvic splanchnic nerve afferents (S2-S4). The male urethra has prostatic, membranous and spongy parts; catheters meet resistance at the membranous part (the external urethral sphincter) and at the bulbous part, where a false passage can be made if force is used.',
 ['Seek urgent medical care. Acute retention needs relief of the bladder.','Do not let the person drink large amounts of fluid while waiting.','Do not try to pass an object into the urethra at home.','A trained clinician passes a urethral catheter gently with lubricant. If this is not possible, a suprapubic catheter is inserted through the lower abdominal wall in the midline, about 2-3 cm above the pubic symphysis, when the bladder is palpable or seen on ultrasound.'],
 'Inability to pass urine with pain, confusion or fever is an emergency.',
 [mc('Which part of the male urethra is surrounded by the prostate?',['Prostatic part','Membranous part','Spongy part','Bulbous part'],0,'The prostatic urethra passes through the prostate; enlargement can compress it.','foundation','Name the parts of the male urethra'),
  mc('Where is a suprapubic catheter inserted?',['Midline, above the pubic symphysis','Left iliac fossa','Perineum','Umbilicus'],0,'It passes through the lower abdominal wall in the midline above the pubic symphysis into a distended bladder, which lies above the pubis (extraperitoneal).','intermediate','Locate the suprapubic site')])
case('em-pelvic-urethral-injury','Pelvic fracture and urethral injury','urinary','urinary',
 'Explain why catheterisation is avoided after a pelvic fracture with blood at the urethral opening.',
 'A cyclist is thrown over his handlebars. His pelvis is painful and unstable, there is bruising of the perineum, and blood is at the tip of the penis.',
 ['hip-bone','male-urethra','urinary-bladder','prostate'],
 'The membranous urethra is fixed at the urogenital diaphragm and is easily torn when the pelvic ring breaks. The bladder can also rupture. Blood at the urethral meatus, a high-riding prostate and perineal bruising suggest urethral injury. A blind catheter may convert a partial tear into a complete one.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Keep the person still and lying flat; do not move the pelvis.','Control bleeding with firm pressure on external wounds. Do not push on the pelvis or rock it.','Do not try to pass a urinary catheter if there is blood at the urethral opening. Doctors perform a urethrogram first or place a suprapubic catheter.','Treat shock: keep warm, monitor breathing and pulse. A pelvic binder is applied by trained staff.'],
 'Any suspected pelvic fracture, especially after high-energy trauma, is a life-threatening emergency.',
 [mc('Which part of the male urethra is most vulnerable in pelvic fractures?',['Membranous part','Prostatic part','Penile part','Navicular fossa'],0,'The membranous urethra is fixed and short, and is torn when the pelvic ring is displaced.','intermediate','Identify the site of urethral injury'),
  tf('A urinary catheter should always be passed at once if the person has pelvic trauma.',False,'False. With blood at the meatus, a blind catheter can worsen the injury; imaging or suprapubic drainage is used.','intermediate','State the catheter rule')])
